{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>And the cloud covered the Tent of Meeting.</b> He says that the cloud covered the Tent from every side, and it was covered and concealed within it." "<b>And the glory of YHWH filled the Mishkan.</b> For its interior was full of the glory, since the glory dwells within the cloud, inside the Mishkan, as it is said concerning Mount Sinai (Exodus 20:21), \"into the thick darkness where Elohim was,\" and he says that Moses could not enter the Tent of Meeting (Exodus 40:35) — not even to the entrance — because the cloud covered it and he was not permitted to come within the cloud. Furthermore, since the Mishkan was full of the glory of YHWH, how could he enter it? The reason is that he was not to come there without permission; rather, He would call him and he would come within the cloud, as he did at Mount Sinai: \"And He called to Moses on the seventh day from within the cloud\" (Exodus 24:16), and it says, \"And Moses came within the cloud\" (v. 18). According to the plain sense, because it is said, \"And YHWH spoke to him from the Tent of Meeting\" (Leviticus 1:1), Moses did not enter the Mishkan; rather, He called him from the Tent of Meeting, and he stood at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting, and He spoke to him. And our Sages said (Sifrei Zuta, introduction 8): one verse says, \"And Moses was not able to enter the Tent of Meeting,\" and another verse says, \"And when Moses came into the Tent of Meeting\" (Numbers 7:89) — the phrase \"for the cloud abode upon it\" decides between them. For according to their opinion, \"And when Moses came into the Tent of Meeting\" means that he would come there without a call, on his own initiative; or because it says there, \"and he heard the Voice speaking to him from above the Kapporet,\" it appears to them that Moses stood inside the Tent before the Kapporet, and at any time that the glory of HaShem filled the Mishkan, Moses did not enter within it. Therefore they say that this was after the cloud had withdrawn — that is, it withdrew from covering the entire Tent, and the glory no longer filled the Mishkan, for this had been so only on the eighth day, when the glory descended there. And the call of which it says, \"And He called to Moses, and YHWH spoke to him from the Tent of Meeting\" (Leviticus 1:1), according to their opinion had taken place beforehand, as I explained above (Ramban on Exodus 40:2). And it is possible that the verse which says once more, <b>and the glory of YHWH filled the Mishkan</b>, alludes to the glory that dwells within it:" "Behold, the Book of Redemption is completed, in which HaShem, Elohim of Israel, came." "To the children of Israel, a people near to Him." "He saved him from the hand of his hater and redeemed him from the hand of his enemy." "And blessed is HaShem, who desires the peace of His servant." "Who until now has helped him to arrive." "Who renews his youth in old age." "Who satisfies his hunger with His Torah." "And fed him honey and milk." "For He prepared all his heart." "And to His name may he bless morning and evening." "Blessed is He from whose portion we have eaten and through whose goodness we have lived."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {1 "The Sages are cited from Sifrei Zuta (ת\"כ); the abbreviation ת\"ק could also refer to Tanna d'Bei Eliyahu, but the reference to פתיחתא ח aligns with Sifrei Zuta's introductory sections."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:23:21.829082457Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}